🐛 Bug
We have a model created, trained and exported by scripting in Python. However, when we are trying to deploy it, it gives totally wrong result on iOS platform. On the other hand, it gives correct result on C++ frontend on my linux devices with exactly same input. After ruling out many other possibilities, we suspect iOS frontend has some bugs triggered by our model.
To Reproduce
Our workflow is as follow:


Train the model in python


Scirpt the model with the following code.


filename_model = '/path/to/serialized/model'
model = torch.load(filename_model, map_location='cpu').eval()
traced_script_module = torch.jit.script(model)
traced_script_module.save("./exported_model.th")


Load and use model in our iOS project. (Irrelevant preprocession and postprocession omitted)
Loading:

@implementation TorchModule {
 @protected
    torch::jit::script::Module _impl;
}

- (nullable instancetype)initWithFileAtPath:(NSString*)filePath {
  self = [super init];
  if (self) {
    try {
      auto qengines = at::globalContext().supportedQEngines();
      if (std::find(qengines.begin(), qengines.end(), at::QEngine::QNNPACK) != qengines.end()) {
        at::globalContext().setQEngine(at::QEngine::QNNPACK);
      }
      _impl = torch::jit::load(filePath.UTF8String);
      _impl.eval();
    } catch (const std::exception& exception) {
      NSLog(@"%s", exception.what());
      return nil;
    }
  }
  return self;
}

Forwarding:
@implementation PCMEvaluateTorchModule {
    @private
    torch::Tensor _tempOnset, _tempFrame;
}

- (void)predict:(void*)buffer frameCount:(int)frameCount frameOutput:(float **)frameOutput onsetOutput:(float **)onsetOutput {
      try {

          torch::Tensor tensor = torch::from_blob(b, {1, 1, frameCount, 229});
          torch::autograd::AutoGradMode guard(false);
          torch::AutoNonVariableTypeMode nfn_var_type_mode(true);

          auto ans = _impl.forward({tensor}).toTuple();
          int subscript = 0;
          for (const auto &element : ans->elements()) {
              if(subscript == 0) // onset
              {
                  _tempOnset = element.toTensor();
              }
              else if(subscript == 3) // frame
              {
                  _tempFrame = element.toTensor();
              }
              subscript++;
          }

          *frameOutput = _tempFrame.data_ptr<float>();
          *onsetOutput = _tempOnset.data_ptr<float>();

      } catch (const std::exception& exception) {
          NSLog(@"%s", exception.what());
      }
}


Our original model's forward() method returns 4 tensors, and that becomes a tuple of 4 tensors in C++ interface.
Expected behavior
Given that the iOS side code is almost completely same to official demo and is relatively simple. We expect the model will give correct result, which should be very close to the results on Python and C++ frontends.
