We'd like to make it an error to def() an operator in the operator registration multiple times (#37010)
However, while attempting #37010, we discovered that there is a site where we are def()'ing an operator a second time:
class Registerer final {
public:
  Registerer&& op(const std::string& schemaStr, KernelFunction::InternalBoxedKernelFunction* boxed_kernel_wrapper) && {
    static auto& dispatcher = c10::Dispatcher::singleton();
    auto schema = parseSchema(schemaStr);
    schema.setAliasAnalysis(AliasAnalysisKind::FROM_SCHEMA);
    c10::OperatorName name = schema.operator_name();
    RegistrationHandleRAII registration = dispatcher.registerDef(std::move(schema), "registered by JIT");
    auto op = dispatcher.findSchema(name).value();
    registrationHandles_.push_back(std::move(registration));
    dispatcher.setManuallyBoxedKernelFor_(op, boxed_kernel_wrapper);
    return std::move(*this);
  }


in tools/jit/templates/generated_unboxing_wrappers.cpp. The invocation of registerDef counts as a second invocation of def (where the first invocation is the "normal" one found in build/aten/src/ATen/TypeDefault.cpp.
In discussion with @smessmer, we think that we can remove the def definition here; instead, we should make it possible to add a variant of registerImpl which doesn't take any implementation, and ensures that the operator handle exists so we can setManuallyBoxedKernelFor on it (without some registerDef/registerImpl call, findSchema will fail.)
Your task:

Read the code in Dispatcher.h/Dispatcher.cpp and form a general mental model of how registration works. This design doc (FB only) https://fb.quip.com/J8i7A65vI8ms may help give you some orientation about some of the design constraints for how we designed dispatcher. Pay particular attention to findOrRegisterName_, and how it is used by registerImpl and registerDef
add a new registerName method, modeled off of registerImpl, except that it only takes an op_name as an argument. It omits the call to op.registerKernel. You'll have to add a corresponding deregisterName_ method modeled off of deregisterImpl_, but once again not calling deregisterKernel.
Modify generated_unboxing_wrappers.cpp to use registerName rather than registerDef. In doing so, you will stop using the schema to refer to the operator, and instead use an operator name. After the modification, you will redundantly take in the schema string in Registerer; this cleanup can be left to a follow up task, or if you like you can see if you can modify call sites fo Registerer to only pass in operator name (as the full schema is no longer necessary)
This is a self contained PR which should pass all CIs. Once it is landed, you could make an attempt at #37010 to see if there are other cases where we are redundantly defining defs that already exist.
